#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166601 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "b301b73e-1d26-56b7-bb21-33d6acae8d5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8557" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(6, 0.7)$, compute $P(X = 2)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(6, 0.7)$, so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{6}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{6-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 2) &= f(2) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{6}{2})(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{6 - 2} \\\\\n&= 15(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{4} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.0595\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "449e6e8f-0e9b-5208-8945-1ce3ea3d3dbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8558" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim B(5, 0.3)$, compute $P(0 < X < 3)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(5, 0.3)$, so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} (0.3)^x (0.7)^{5-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 < X < 3) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2}) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{5}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5 - 1} + (\\frac{5}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= 5(0.3)(0.7)^{4} + 10(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.6689\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "ae2b59a4-d3d6-53ea-aca2-93cbb2da7478" :question/math-academy-id "e-8559" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(7, 0.2)$, compute $P(X \\geq 2)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(7, 0.2)$, so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{7}{x} (0.2)^x (0.8)^{7-x}\n$$\n\nComputing\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2)\n$$\n\nwill require us to compute $f(x)$ at\n\n$$\nx=2, 3, 4, \\ldots, 7\n$$\n\nHowever, if we compute the complement $P(X < 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0, 1$, which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X < 2)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 2) &= P(X ∈ {0, 1}) \\\\\n&= f(0) + f(1) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{7 - 0} + (\\frac{7}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{7 - 1} \\\\\n&= (0.8)^{7} + 7(0.2)(0.8)^{6} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.5767\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 2) &= 1 - P(X < 2) \\\\\n&\\approx 1 - 0.5767 \\\\\n&= 0.4233\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "aeaecf43-9fce-552c-a3f8-69a2f09f2d9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8564" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(10, 0.7)$, compute $P(X > 8)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(10, 0.7)$, so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{10}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{10-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 8) &= P(X ∈ {9, 10}) \\\\\n&= f(9) + f(10) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10 - 9} + (\\frac{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10 - 10} \\\\\n&= 10(0.7)^{9}(0.3) + 1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1493\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b2e102-cb56-5e08-b77f-7abec5470b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166051} {:answer/id #uuid "010db3d6-a014-5d17-8548-d15e9a8ba1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1274" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166052} {:answer/id #uuid "0153ac21-e4fe-570d-850e-96f9991d89cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0825" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166053} {:answer/id #uuid "2cae1324-5459-55e2-97b7-d72626cc2e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166054} {:answer/id #uuid "67236a07-54e5-53a8-8563-cff732445f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80b2e102-cb56-5e08-b77f-7abec5470b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :